WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:10.010
Democrats rightly attack Republicans when
they promise their math-defying tax-cut plans

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:13.380
will slash government revenue but somehow
not balloon deficits.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:18.520
Well, what tax cuts are to Republicans, single-payer
health care seems to be for Democrats: a populist

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:22.370
policy idea that's far more attractive in
theory than practice.

00:00:22.370 --> 00:00:24.230
&nbsp;
Bernie Sanders’ Medicare for All plan is

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:28.369
the perfect example: Recall that economists
sharply criticized his presidential campaign's

00:00:28.369 --> 00:00:33.030
health-care plan for severely underestimating
the costs of the benefits it offered.

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:37.030
It turned out the taxes needed to pay for
BernieCare were twice as high as estimated.

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:38.920
&nbsp;
His plan would also phase out employer-provided

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:43.749
health care, even though some 70 percent of
workers in those plans say they're satisfied

00:00:43.749 --> 00:00:44.749
with their coverage.

00:00:44.749 --> 00:00:49.780
In other words, the Sanders plan fails to
recognize either economic or political reality,

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:51.819
similar to many Republican tax cut promises.

00:00:51.819 --> 00:00:53.559
The economy is doing pretty well right now.

00:00:53.559 --> 00:00:57.879
Now is hardly time to gamble on ill-considered
populist schemes from the left or right.

00:00:57.879 --> 00:01:00.749
&nbsp;
How about this instead: Stabilize ObamaCare.

00:01:00.749 --> 00:01:03.450
Reform the tax code to encourage more domestic
investment.

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:21.530
And do both in a bipartisan, fiscally responsible
way.

